  "limitations": [
    "Study 1 used a quasi-experimental design, which made self-selection of individuals into groups a problem, despite controls (p. 6, 9).",
    "Study 1 had restricted population sampling (experienced, physically fit backpackers), limiting external validity (p. 13).",
    "Physiological measures in Study 2 were not monitored during the treatment experiences, only after, potentially missing immediate recovery effects (p. 18-19).",
    "Final physiological assessments in Study 2 were obtained approximately 50 minutes after task completion, which may have been too late to detect differences (p. 18-19).",
    "The perceived restorativeness scale used in Study 2 is in its initial stage of development, tested with a modest sample and limited range of settings (p. 23).",
    "The immediate emotional response after a wilderness experience in Study 1 was mixed, sometimes including negative feelings about returning to daily life (p. 15, 22).",
    "The longer-term positive emotional effect found in Study 1 is a tentative finding and requires more extended investigation (p. 22)."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This paper presents two empirical studies investigating the restorative effects of natural environment experiences. Study 1, a quasi-experimental field study, compared wilderness backpacking to non-wilderness vacations and a control group, finding that backpacking led to increased overall happiness and improved proofreading performance (a measure of mental fatigue restoration) in the long term. Study 2, a true experiment, randomly assigned college students to a nature walk, an urban walk, or passive relaxation, demonstrating that the nature walk resulted in higher perceived restorativeness, increased positive affect, decreased anger/aggression, and better proofreading performance. While physiological measures did not show significant differences in Study 2, both studies provided convergent evidence from self-reports and cognitive performance for nature's restorative benefits. Limitations include the quasi-experimental nature and restricted sample of Study 1, and the delayed physiological measurements and early stage of the restorativeness scale in Study 2.",
